  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSimilar to the past few days, our region remains a warm and\nhumid air mass on the western side of the Bermuda High.\nTemperatures has reached well into the 80s for most the region\nwith dewpoints in the 60s to low 70s. Although PWATs are\nslightly lower today compared to yesterday on the 12z KALY\nsounding (1.62\" vs. 1.90\" last evening), they remain above-\nnormal. Latest SPC mesoanalysis shows abundant instability in\nplace, with MLCAPE values around 1000 J/kg, with locally around\n1500 J/kg across far southern areas. Even higher instability is\nlocated south of the region over eastern PA and NJ. Mid-level\nlapse rates are a little higher compared to yesterday due to\napproaching height falls (around 6 deg C/km), but this is still\nisn't too impressive at this time. 0-6 km bulk shear is\ngenerally only around 20 kts or so, so not too high either. \n\nAlthough there is a subtle upper level disturbance moving \nacross the eastern Great Lakes, overall large-scale forcing is \nrather weak and the nearest surface boundary is well west of the\narea over the Midwest. Still, this disturbance has been enough \nto allow for some scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop\nwest and southwest of the region over central NY and especially\ncentral and eastern PA, where some severe storms have been \nongoing. While the 3km HRRR doesn't seem to be handling this \nactivity very well at the moment, radar trends would suggest \nthat the bulk of this activity may slide south of the region. \nHowever, southern areas (such as the Catskills, mid Hudson \nValley, Taconics, and parts of western New England), could be \nimpacted by some of this activity later this afternoon and into \nthis evening. Isolated damaging wind gusts can't be ruled out \ndue to the unstable environment, but the weak lapse rates and \nlimited large-scale forcing should keep severe storms from being\noverly widespread. \n\nThe other threat will be locally heavy downpours. Similar to\nyesterday, the humid air mass will be capable of producing heavy\ndownpours in a short period of time, with 1 to 2 inches of\nrainfall easily falling in any thunderstorm. If this occurs\nover an urban area or location that has seen recent rainfall, \nit could quickly cause a flash flood. Will need to monitor radar\ntrends through this evening for any flash flood potential,\nespecially if storms repeat over the same areas. At this time,\nsouthern areas may be most as risk based on radar trends, but\nreally can't rule it out for anywhere in the forecast area.\nStorms should wind down by the late evening hours with the loss\nof daytime heating and the threat for flooding downpours will be\ndone by midnight or so.\n\nOn Saturday, there may again be the threat for isolated storms,\nalthough coverage looks lower than today. 0-6 km bulk shear will\ncontinue to be on the lower side on Saturday (mainly under 30\nkts), although it will continue to be rather warm and muggy.\nIsolated wind damage or a isolated flash flooding cannot be\nruled out once again, but the sparser storm coverage should keep\nthese threats more limited for Saturday. The main time window\nfor Saturday will be afternoon into the early evening hours\nbased on the latest CAMs. Luckily, drier weather is expected for\nSunday, with no threat for strong/severe storms or flooding\ndownpours for Sunday at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHeat Advisory continues through 8 pm this evening for eastern\nUlster, western Dutchess and southern Litchfield Counties.\nDewpoints remain near 70 in these areas, with afternoon temps\nreaching the mid to upper 80s. This should allow for heat index\nto exceed 95 degrees for a few hours for the mid afternoon\nthrough early evening hours. Residents and visitors should\npractice heat safety, and take breaks in the shade and drink\nplenty of water to avoid heat-illness in these areas. Elsewhere,\nit will continue to be warm and muggy as well, with heat index\nvalues in the mid 80s to low 90s.\n\nOn Saturday, it will be warm and humid once again. Heat Index\nvalues look to stay just below advisory, but will likely be in\nthe upper 80s to low 90s for many valley areas. Dewpoints will\nstart to fall on Sunday as a cold front moves through the area,\nwhich should allow for a brief improvement in the heat index\nvalues for late in the weekend into early next week.",
